description.p:
The other Handshaking expansions can be implemented similarly, but they are both somewhat bigger. For this reshuffling, the validity and neutrality of the output data R implies the neutrality of the input data L. Logic which has this property is called "weak-condition" . It means that the L does not need to be checked anywhere else, besides in R. The WCHB also gets some of its semicolons implemented for free. 

The semicolon between If ; 

 is implemented by the environment, as is the implicit semicolon at the end of the loop. The WCHB has some inherent benefits. However, it turns out that although WCHB works well for buffers, the "weak- condition" requirement can cause problems with other circuits.

] 

 Note that the resetting of the output channels X and Y must accommodate the cases when those channels were not used. Since they produce no outputs, they must not wait for the acknowledges. Adding in the 

 terms will allow the wait to be completed vacuously. This does not actually generate any production rules. This Handshaking expansion can be compiled into production rules, but there are some tricky details.
